# amont-agent-cases-v1
# Reviewed judgements for this rule. Generated by
#   amont-agent explain <rule> --format cases
# then labelled by hand. `amont-agent corpus check` replays every line through
# the rules as they stand today, and runs as a test — so widening a rule in a
# way that breaks a judgement somebody already made is a red build.
#
# match   the rule SHOULD fire here
# nomatch the rule must stay silent here — these are the ones that matter,
#         because a corpus of positives alone measures recall, not precision
# ?       nobody has looked yet; never counts as evidence
#
# These label the SHAPE only. `corpus check` replays `examine`, which is pure
# and cannot know where the command runs — so several `match` lines below are
# commands in a linked worktree, which are correct behaviour and which
# `confirm` silences at run time ("already in a linked worktree"). That split
# is deliberate: the corpus guards the shape boundary, and the location is a
# fact about the repository that only `confirm` may go and look up.

# --- the rule should fire (from real transcripts) ---
match	git checkout -b chore/publish-tooling origin/main 2>&1 | tail -1
match	git fetch origin -q && git checkout -b feat/duro-cli origin/main 2>&1 | tail -1 && git log --oneline -1
match	git checkout -q -B chore/release-1-10-0 origin/main && sed -n 9,50p CHANGELOG.md | head -45
match	git checkout -b feat/phase5b-source-flips forgejo/main 2>&1 | tail -3 && git log --oneline -1
match	cd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/application-landscape-wt-pb2-m1 && git checkout -b feat/app-services && git branch --show-current
match	cd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/duro-app-wt-git-access && git switch -C feat/device-qr-claim origin/main 2>&1 | tail -1
match	cd /Users/fredericrous/Developer/Perso/gt-conf && git reset -q --hard dca6dba && git status --short

# --- the rule must stay silent ---
# Navigating the shared checkout back to its default branch is what you do
# when you are FINISHED. Firing here would put the rule in front of tidying up.
nomatch	git checkout main
nomatch	git switch main
# `git worktree add -b` is the remedy this rule recommends. A rule whose own
# advice trips it is unobeyable.
nomatch	git fetch origin -q && git worktree add ../amont-wt-thing -b fix/z origin/main
nomatch	git worktree add ../application-landscape-wt-flows -b feat/e2e-flows origin/main
# `checkout --` restores files; it creates no branch and moves no HEAD.
nomatch	git checkout -- src/lib.rs
# Only `--hard` discards a working tree. `--soft` and the default `--mixed`
# leave it alone, and a naive `git reset` match would have swept both in.
nomatch	git reset --soft HEAD~1
nomatch	git reset HEAD src/lib.rs
# `--detach` creates no branch.
nomatch	git checkout --detach origin/main
# A branch name out of a substitution cannot be quoted back at anyone.
nomatch	git checkout -b $(date +%s)
